Please strictly refer to the original image of the character I uploaded to create a **1:1 square transparent sticker sheet**.

【Screen Structure】
- The entire image consists of **9 independent stickers**;
- Arranged in a **3×3 grid**;
- Keep **wide and completely transparent gaps** between the nine stickers;
- The background of the entire sticker sheet must be transparent;
- Do not generate additional backgrounds, do not make it into a single continuous illustration, and do not let the stickers overlap.

【Subject Consistency】
- All nine stickers must be the **same character**;
- Strictly preserve the identity characteristics, face shape, facial features, hairstyle, hair color, skin color, key clothing features, and overall temperament of the character in the original image;
- Slight optimizations can be made, such as improving clarity, optimizing edges, and fine-tuning textures;
- Do not change the character's identity, do not swap faces, and do not make different stickers look like different people.

【Center Sticker Requirements】
- The **middle 5th cell** is fixed as a cutout version of the character from the original image;
- The center sticker should maintain realism, with only slight refinement;
- Do not apply stylization to the center sticker;
- The center sticker should form a "real vs. stylized" contrast with the surrounding eight stickers.

【Surrounding Eight Stickers Requirements】
- The surrounding eight stickers are all based on the extension of the same character;
- Uniformly converted into the following style:
Extract the most recognizable subjects, outlines, gestures, and narrative relationships from the photos, and reconstruct them into observational pen and wash manuscripts. Do not fully trace the photos; only keep the visual clues most worth recording, maintaining the subject's identity, proportions, directions, and scene meanings with minimal information, so that the original object can be recognized at a glance, while retaining traces of observation, deletion, and stopping the pen.

The composition emphasizes a musical rhythm within a minimalist page: treat the most important outline or movement of the subject as a visual motif, organizing the image through repetition, variation, pauses, accents, and sudden white space. The subject does not need to be complete, nor does it need to be centered; it can be offset, cropped, suspended, or naturally extended in a certain direction; one or two powerful free lines carry the visual accent, while other lines quickly fade away, letting large areas of blank space act like rests in music. Avoid even distribution and full filling, making the image present a sense of improvisation within a restrained order and completeness within the unfinished.

Lines use slender, loose, uneven hand-drawn pen outlines, allowing for broken contours, searching lines, repeated corrections, sudden weight, slight deviations, and unfinished edges. Some areas can feature freer, more decisive long lines or fast brushstrokes, forming a rhythmic contrast with quiet fine lines, but key proportions, directions, and gestures must be accurate. Light colors are only used in a few necessary positions, extracting 2–4 most recognizable colors from the original image for light, transparent local dotting, allowing for bleeding, water marks, graininess, and uneven coverage, with environmental colors disappearing quickly.

The background paper color is not fixed; intelligently match an extremely light, clean, almost white composite background based on the original image's overall color temperature, light, and mood, which can be slightly warm, slightly cool, or have a very light hue, but always serves the original image and does not form a distinct colored background.

Text is freely generated based on the photo itself, without preset fixed titles, numbers, locations, years, or fixed languages. A small amount of truly meaningful words, phrases, short sentences, or symbols can be refined from the subject, movement, environment, emotion, sound, sense of time, memory, or accidental details. The text also follows the rhythm of the image, entering the white space like a slight accent, pause, or echo, naturally arranged along outlines, lines of sight, movement directions, or unfinished edges; it is better to be few, offset, light, and clever than to form a menu-style information area.

【Overall Effect】
- The middle one is the realistic cutout version of the original image;
- The surrounding eight are stylized meme stickers of the same character;
- All stickers should have the feel of independent PNG stickers, with clear edges and complete subjects;
- The overall look is like a pre-formatted nine-grid transparent meme sticker sheet.

Please turn each uploaded travel photo into an independent high-end 3:4 vertical travel scrapbook work; do not combine multiple photos into one image.

The screen adopts a clear top-bottom structure:

The upper part occupies about 50% of the screen, completely retaining the input real photo. Maintain the identity of the subject, the number and posture of people, architectural structure, road direction, spatial relationship, real materials, natural light and shadow, and original colors. Do not change locations, add or delete people, reshape buildings, change perspective, or turn photos into illustrations. Only slight travel magazine-level color grading and restrained film texture are allowed.

The lower part occupies about 50% of the screen, showing a real open travel scrapbook. The scrapbook is placed on a low-saturation dark desktop, clearly showing the left and right pages, the central spine, the thickness of the paper pages, the slight curling arc of the pages, natural shadows, and fine paper texture. Black binder clips can be used on both sides of the book to fix it.

The left page uses a low-saturation paper extracted from the colors of the photo as the base page, and a torn-edge printed photo of the same travel photo is pasted on it. The photo must remain recognizable as the original scene but can be re-cropped to highlight the most important subject. The torn edges should have real paper fibers, slight warping, and natural thickness, and be fixed with two or three small pieces of layered beige tape.

The right page maintains a large area of continuous warm white paper; do not fill it with content. Based on information actually visible or reliably confirmed in the input photo, select two to four small commemorative elements, such as:

- Architectural details, windows, arches, roofs, street lamps, or tile patterns;
- Vehicle license plates, street signs, tickets, or transportation route segments;
- Water surfaces, plants, boats, bridges, sculptures, or local material textures;
- A very small torn-edge photo, a piece of graph paper, or a short pencil sketch.

Commemorative elements must come from the input photo or confirmed location information; do not add landmarks of other cities, generic travel stickers, or fictional souvenirs. All small elements must be significantly smaller than the main photo on the left page and distributed loosely around the text, keeping the right page quiet, restrained, and breathable.

The right page only includes the following three layers of English text:

1. A title of 1–3 English words extracted based on the photo subject or location;
2. A short field in the format "PLACE / FIELD NOTE"; if the location cannot be reliably determined, use "TRAVEL / FIELD NOTE";
3. An observation sentence of 5–12 English words, only describing the colors, light, architecture, roads, natural scenery, or spatial feelings actually visible in the photo.

The text must be short, accurate, and clearly readable, using dark brown pencil, pen, or natural handwritten texture. Do not generate long paragraphs, and do not add author names, dates, coordinates, brands, websites, QR codes, logos, garbled characters, fake Chinese, or other text.

The overall style is like a private commemorative scrapbook organized after a real trip: quiet, warm, restrained, with the tactile feel of real paper, tape, torn-edge photos, and collectibles. The top is the real world, and the bottom is the paper memory left from the same place.

Must maintain:

- One input photo corresponds to one independent finished product;
- 3:4 vertical format;
- Top and bottom areas are close to 1:1;
- The top photo is clear, complete, and highly faithful;
- The bottom must be a real open scrapbook, not a flat double-column poster;
- The left page carries the main photo;
- The right page maintains a large area of white space;
- Commemorative elements must come from the same place;
- All image content can be traced back to the input photo.

Avoid: multiple-photo collage, grid layout, altered architecture, invented landmark, replaced subject, distorted perspective, mirrored extension, blurred-photo background, dense scrapbook, excessive stickers, generic travel icons, unrelated souvenirs, full-page decoration, glossy digital template, flat vector layout, plastic 3D, illegible text, excessive typography, fake logo, watermark, signature.

